WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:03.040
A tiny woman against a hulking alien nightmare?

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.290
A CGI bear that looks absolutely unbearable?

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.834
Some Pred-heads are really gunning for Prey.

00:00:08.919 --> 00:00:13.320
By far the biggest complaint among fans is
the controversial redesign of the Predator,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.300
which portrays the creature in a decidedly
more monstrous form than we're used to.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.339
Dubbed "the Feral Predator," it's an ancestor
to the beast seen fighting Arnold Schwarzenegger's

00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:25.380
Dutch in the original film, 300 years after
the events of "Prey."

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.819
Director Dan Trachtenberg told Digital Spy,

00:00:27.819 --> 00:00:32.830
"I wanted to make this version of the Predator
so much more feral and ferocious and animalistic,

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.750
from its movements to its silhouette and its
design."

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.840
While Trachtenberg's explanation makes sense,
some "Predator" fans have responded negatively

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.050
to the new look, which is revealed during
an action scene midway through the film.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:48.070
You don't need to look further than the comments
section on the fan website AVP Galaxy to see

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.890
the disdain many have for the design.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.900
For instance, one user said,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.930
"Feral's weapons were interesting and it was
fun watching him on screen.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.760
My one gripe is that I wasn't crazy about
the face redesign.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.620
It was just okay for me."

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.560
Similar sentiments appear in various comment
circles across the web, though the new look

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.580
does have several fans.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.110
One user is quoted as saying,

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.390
"'Prey' is a solid #predator movie.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.050
Predator redesign seemed unnecessary, but
didn't bother me.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:13.447
Way better than expected."

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:17.960
The predator seen in "Prey" lives up to the
species' storied reputation.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.960
The big guy hunts and kills a rattlesnake,
a wolf, a bear, a group of Comanches, and

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.670
a whole squad of French trappers in brutal
fashion.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.130
These scenes offer some of the best action
of the entire "Predator" franchise, as the

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:32.950
alien baddie utilizes an assortment of unique
weapons and abilities to take out his foes.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:37.210
Perhaps that's why some fans were left feeling
short changed at the monster's demise.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:41.920
Following his trail of carnage, the hunter
chases Naru, who stole his mask in a previous

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.760
scene, into the forest and eventually becomes
stuck in some mud.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:48.560
He disappears below the surface but manages
to pull himself out of the trap long enough

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.580
to aim his arrow launcher at Naru's head,
unaware that the clever warrior has placed

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.590
his mask on a nearby tree.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.880
"This is as far as you go.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.020
No more.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.570
This is it."

00:02:02.166 --> 00:02:06.517
Our antagonist can only tilt his head in confusion
before an arrow streaks through his skull,

00:02:06.517 --> 00:02:07.777
killing him instantly.

00:02:07.777 --> 00:02:08.997
A Twitter user noted,

00:02:08.997 --> 00:02:13.137
"The Predator dies in what I think is the
dumbest way for a Predator to die . . . It

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:17.688
would be a badass kill if he didn't stand
there looking all 'oh s--' for a few good seconds."

00:02:17.688 --> 00:02:21.866
Another complaint about "Prey" among fans
is the Predator's inability to kill a young

00:02:21.866 --> 00:02:23.707
woman with minimal combat experience.

00:02:23.707 --> 00:02:27.947
One user made a composite image of the entire
cast of the original "Predator" film standing

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:31.987
next to Naru, indicating that it took an entire
squad of trained soldiers to take down the

00:02:31.987 --> 00:02:36.817
original monster, but only a single, inexperienced
warrior to succeed in the latest film.

00:02:36.817 --> 00:02:40.927
One could argue the point of Naru's character
arc is to show the merits of brains over brawn

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.117
in outwitting an interstellar killing machine.

00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:46.947
That isn't convincing enough for some, including
one Twitter user, who states,

00:02:46.947 --> 00:02:52.336
"In this movie the predator 1 punch kills
a 1k pound grizzly but can't strangle the

00:02:52.336 --> 00:02:55.287
110-pound protag even when it has its hand
around her neck."

00:02:55.287 --> 00:03:00.067
One could also point out that a theme of the
entire original "Predator" film is "brains over brawn."

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:03.947
Dutch is the last one standing because he
realizes that the combat experience and physical

00:03:03.947 --> 00:03:08.037
might of his team are no match for the kind
of clever strategy that both he and Naru rely

00:03:08.037 --> 00:03:09.667
on in their climactic battles.

00:03:10.577 --> 00:03:12.497
"Prey" is not a perfect film.

00:03:12.497 --> 00:03:16.467
There's plenty to love, but this "Predator"
prequel has some glaring issues - notably,

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:17.567
the wonky CGI.

00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:22.557
Many of the animals in the movie look a little
off - especially during the bear fight.

00:03:22.557 --> 00:03:25.786
Even fans who like the film point out that
some of the visual effects in this sequence

00:03:25.786 --> 00:03:28.927
are simply not up to modern blockbuster standards.

00:03:28.927 --> 00:03:29.927
One critic said,

00:03:29.927 --> 00:03:32.567
"A couple of intro shots of the bear were
not very good.

00:03:32.567 --> 00:03:36.767
Later on when the bear and Predator were fighting
was much much better, but you can tell they

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:40.927
were like, 'This part doesn't matter all that
much, but this next part will be in the trailer.'

00:03:40.927 --> 00:03:42.116
It was a little rough."

00:03:42.116 --> 00:03:43.116
Another tweeted,

00:03:43.116 --> 00:03:47.457
"Prey was basically 'what if the power rangers
movie had very bad CGI?'"

00:03:47.457 --> 00:03:51.097
Though that same user went on to praise the
action scenes overall, they clearly had no

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:53.500
love for the distracting special effects.

00:03:53.622 --> 00:03:59.497
Naru is largely seen as a misfit by her fellow
tribesmen who don't support her rebellious ways.

00:03:59.497 --> 00:04:03.317
In fact, except for her brother Taabe, no
one steps up to support Naru or her quest

00:04:03.317 --> 00:04:04.967
to become a great hunter.

00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:07.586
"Who invited you?

00:04:07.586 --> 00:04:09.987
We won't be gone long enough to need a cook."

00:04:09.987 --> 00:04:14.927
But the understatement of this aspect is one
of the reasons some critics praised "Prey,"

00:04:14.927 --> 00:04:15.927
with one noting,

00:04:15.927 --> 00:04:19.887
"The subtle approach to the arc of Naru is
appreciated as [the filmmakers] rarely resort

00:04:19.887 --> 00:04:23.447
to forced antagonism via the tribespeople
to make their point."

00:04:23.447 --> 00:04:27.507
Some fans don't agree, including the hosts
of The Angry Movie Review, who cite the scene

00:04:27.507 --> 00:04:31.107
in which one of the men in Naru's tribe orders
her to go back to the village.

00:04:31.107 --> 00:04:35.127
The critic argues that while the explicit
bullying makes for easy conflict, it also

00:04:35.127 --> 00:04:36.397
feels a little dumbed down.

00:04:36.397 --> 00:04:40.547
The others on the show agreed, feeling that
while the overall purpose of the scene was

00:04:40.547 --> 00:04:43.397
fine, the silly dialogue undercuts the tension,
saying,

00:04:43.397 --> 00:04:47.327
"Instead of hokey dialogue, it could have
all been done in looks or different ways.

00:04:47.327 --> 00:04:48.707
Just adult it up a little bit."

00:04:49.397 --> 00:04:54.397
"Prey" ends much like the original "Predator,"
with Naru defeating the alien hunter by utilizing

00:04:54.397 --> 00:04:56.447
her wits and escaping with her life.

00:04:56.447 --> 00:04:59.677
Some have complained that Naru should have
been roughed up a bit more during her battle

00:04:59.677 --> 00:05:03.477
with the Predator, noting that even Arnold
Schwarzenegger was left bloodied and broken

00:05:03.477 --> 00:05:05.197
at the end of the original film.

00:05:05.197 --> 00:05:10.067
One user makes the point that even after she
massacres the alien, Naru doesn't have a mark on her.

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:13.237
They go on to note that the team from "Predator"
had difficulty bringing down the original

00:05:13.237 --> 00:05:15.097
alien even with their high-tech weaponry.

00:05:15.097 --> 00:05:19.237
A Reddit user stated that they enjoyed "Prey,"
but added that they missed how clever the

00:05:19.237 --> 00:05:20.607
predator used to be.

00:05:20.607 --> 00:05:24.207
They went on to lament how the Predators no
longer seem like much of a threatening presence

00:05:24.207 --> 00:05:27.640
after a few movies that have seen them be
beaten with relative ease.

00:05:27.811 --> 00:05:33.307
An aspect of "Prey" drawing criticism from
fans is the lack of memorable supporting characters.

00:05:33.307 --> 00:05:36.847
It's almost a tradition for "Predator" films
to feature great ensembles surrounding the

00:05:36.847 --> 00:05:39.897
central protagonist in their battle with the
alien hunter.

00:05:39.897 --> 00:05:44.987
John McTiernan's original 1987 film included
the likes of Carl Weathers, Bill Duke, Sonny

00:05:44.987 --> 00:05:47.447
Landham, and Jesse Ventura, among others.

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:52.117
While later sequels arguably dipped in quality
from the original, Hit or Miss TV host Andrew

00:05:52.117 --> 00:05:55.517
Tate opined that even the lesser films had
solid supporting casts.

00:05:55.517 --> 00:05:56.517
He argues,

00:05:56.517 --> 00:06:00.647
"Prey doesn't have supporting characters other
than the brother - the lead character's brother.

00:06:00.647 --> 00:06:03.837
He's in it - I thought he was really good
- but he wasn't in it enough.

00:06:03.837 --> 00:06:05.397
There wasn't enough development."

00:06:05.397 --> 00:06:08.526
Tate felt none of the deaths in "Prey" really
mattered, explaining,

00:06:08.526 --> 00:06:12.186
"There's too many other characters that get
wiped out and I'm just like, 'Oh well, it's

00:06:12.186 --> 00:06:14.127
just another generic NPC from Skyrim.

00:06:14.127 --> 00:06:15.737
I don't care about them.'"

00:06:15.737 --> 00:06:19.967
One four out of ten rating on IMDb had a fairly
scathing review, stating,

00:06:19.967 --> 00:06:23.896
"The Comanche people portrayed in the film
have only one thing to live for - survival

00:06:23.896 --> 00:06:27.436
. . . It also makes it very hard for them
to be personable and likable.

00:06:27.436 --> 00:06:31.466
Every character in the film other than the
main girl might as well have been the exact same person."

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:36.147
One of Arnold Schwarzenegger's most iconic
one-liners comes from the original "Predator."

00:06:36.147 --> 00:06:40.167
"If it bleeds, we can kill it."

00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:44.047
The same line is repeated in "Prey" by Naru's
brother Taabe, while they're both tied to

00:06:44.047 --> 00:06:47.277
a tree during the battle sequence with the
French fur trappers.

00:06:47.277 --> 00:06:51.086
For some, the moment was a goosebump-inducing
callback to "Predator," but others felt the

00:06:51.086 --> 00:06:53.716
bit of dialogue felt shoehorned into the film.

00:06:53.716 --> 00:06:56.917
One tweet described the line as being shoehorned
into Prey for no reason.

00:06:56.917 --> 00:06:59.147
In the comment section below the tweet, the
user posited,

00:06:59.147 --> 00:07:02.167
"It would have been better if they gave one
of Billy's lines to them.

00:07:02.167 --> 00:07:05.697
'There's something hunting us, and it ain't
no man . . . we're all gonna die.'

00:07:05.697 --> 00:07:07.471
And then they all indeed die."

00:07:07.711 --> 00:07:11.827
About midway through "Prey," Naru comes to
a startling conclusion while tied up to a

00:07:11.827 --> 00:07:16.187
tree with her brother: the Predator won't
kill her because it doesn't see her as a threat.

00:07:16.187 --> 00:07:18.037
This ties into the overall theme of "Prey."

00:07:18.037 --> 00:07:21.817
Naru is trying to prove her worth to those
around her, while many in her tribe see her

00:07:21.817 --> 00:07:24.577
as little more than someone who needs to learn
her place.

00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:27.247
However, some fans were angered by this twist.

00:07:27.247 --> 00:07:29.837
In their negative review of the film, Bad
Movie Reviews claimed,

00:07:29.837 --> 00:07:34.217
"The movie constantly panders to gender politics
as apparently, in this movie, the Predator

00:07:34.217 --> 00:07:37.917
isn't attacking the female lead because it
doesn't see her as a threat . . . Since when

00:07:37.917 --> 00:07:40.716
does the Predator care about the gender of
its victims?"

00:07:40.716 --> 00:07:44.086
In the comment section for the same video,
a user noted that the Predator slaughtered

00:07:44.086 --> 00:07:49.537
the fatally injured fur trapper Raphael despite
knowing he didn't have a weapon and was physically hampered.

00:07:49.537 --> 00:07:50.537
He explains,

00:07:50.537 --> 00:07:54.137
"He certainly wasn't a threat, but that didn't
stop the predator from killing him."

00:07:54.137 --> 00:07:56.497
Others in the comments agree with another
user arguing,

00:07:56.497 --> 00:08:00.746
"Don't let anyone fool you, Predators have
attacked women with weapons in other movies.

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:04.600
Only exception was [Predator 2], where the
woman was pregnant and he didn't."

00:08:04.719 --> 00:08:09.127
In one particular way, "Prey" seems to be
messing with "Predator" franchise lore, which

00:08:09.127 --> 00:08:11.617
is a bad idea when dealing with hardcore fandom.

00:08:11.617 --> 00:08:16.227
The character of Raphael Adolini plays a huge
part in the franchise, at least in terms of

00:08:16.227 --> 00:08:18.007
comic books and spinoffs.

00:08:18.007 --> 00:08:21.556
Adolini was the name inscribed on the gun
given to Danny Glover by one of the departing

00:08:21.556 --> 00:08:25.647
Predators at the end of "Predator 2," seemingly
as a sign of respect.

00:08:25.647 --> 00:08:29.656
For years, fans wondered who this mysterious
man was and why a Predator would carry around

00:08:29.656 --> 00:08:34.886
his weapon, clearly seeing it as some sort
of prized trophy to be handed off when bested in combat.

00:08:34.886 --> 00:08:39.976
As it turns out, the answers lie in a 1996
Dark Horse comic book titled "Predator: 1718,"

00:08:39.976 --> 00:08:42.747
a storyline "Prey" completely ignores.

00:08:42.747 --> 00:08:46.716
Instead, the film features a man named Raphael,
who is killed by the Predator after handing

00:08:46.716 --> 00:08:48.737
his flintlock pistol to Naru.

00:08:48.737 --> 00:08:52.377
While it's neat to see the "Predator 2" Easter
Egg in "Prey," the complete disregard for

00:08:52.377 --> 00:08:57.446
previous comic book canon has disappointed
some, much like Disney's retconning of pre-existing

00:08:57.446 --> 00:08:59.541
"Star Wars" Expanded Universe stories.

00:08:59.954 --> 00:09:04.096
One of the coolest scenes in "Prey" features
Naru taking out a group of French trappers

00:09:04.096 --> 00:09:07.216
using her throwing axe and some well-timed
flips.

00:09:07.216 --> 00:09:11.606
Shot to look like one long take, the sequence
is violent, gory, and completely badass as

00:09:11.606 --> 00:09:15.016
Naru slices and dices enemies left and right
without remorse.

00:09:15.016 --> 00:09:18.476
Still, there were quite a few fans who decried
the moment as unbelievable.

00:09:18.476 --> 00:09:22.797
A Reddit user noted that Naru was mostly seen
as "useless" by her tribe for much of the

00:09:22.797 --> 00:09:24.197
film, continuing,

00:09:24.197 --> 00:09:28.177
"Then when the movie calls for it she can
suddenly slaughter a camp of 10 or so men

00:09:28.177 --> 00:09:32.796
in 45 seconds . . . It would have been a better
step imo for her to have been a capable hunter

00:09:32.796 --> 00:09:38.356
and warrior . . . rather than making her instantly
go into beast mode for the last 15-20 minutes."

00:09:38.356 --> 00:09:42.067
Others have joked that Naru's abilities are
more akin to Keanu Reeves' killer assassin

00:09:42.067 --> 00:09:45.166
in "John Wick" than a real-life Indigenous
warrior.

00:09:45.166 --> 00:09:46.166
One tweet jokes,

00:09:46.166 --> 00:09:50.627
"Naru in 'Prey' is very obviously the founder
of the assassins guild in John Wick.

00:09:50.627 --> 00:09:54.726
The obvious fact that John Wick, Predator,
&amp; Alien is a shared universe is basically

00:09:54.726 --> 00:09:57.709
the greatest gift 2022 could have given me."

